WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.815
Strong algorithms cannot compensate for
exposed key material.

2
00:00:03.815 --> 00:00:06.643
Keep production keys out of source code,

3
00:00:06.643 --> 00:00:09.373
repositories, browser bundles, URLs, logs,
configuration

4
00:00:09.373 --> 00:00:12.591
dumps, analytics, crash diagnostics, and
test snapshots.

5
00:00:12.591 --> 00:00:15.321
A TypeScript alias for an encryption key

6
00:00:15.321 --> 00:00:18.246
organizes code but does not provide
confidentiality.

7
00:00:18.246 --> 00:00:21.756
An application secret embedded in frontend
code is delivered

8
00:00:21.756 --> 00:00:25.364
to an environment controlled by its user;
browser cryptography

9
00:00:25.364 --> 00:00:29.069
does not make that embedded server style
secret private.

10
00:00:29.069 --> 00:00:33.554
Prefer workload identity and narrowly
scoped access to managed key services.

11
00:00:33.554 --> 00:00:37.065
Use a secret manager when direct key
material is

12
00:00:37.065 --> 00:00:40.867
unavoidable, and restrict which operations
each service may perform.

13
00:00:40.867 --> 00:00:44.085
Environment variables can deliver a
secret, but are

14
00:00:44.085 --> 00:00:47.790
not inherently secure storage or a
complete lifecycle system.

15
00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:51.008
Separate development, test, staging, and
production keys.

16
00:00:51.008 --> 00:00:54.908
Production must fail safely when its
approved key is unavailable

17
00:00:54.908 --> 00:00:59.003
rather than falling back to a hard coded
development value.

18
00:00:59.003 --> 00:01:03.001
Log only the metadata needed for
operations, such as approved

19
00:01:03.001 --> 00:01:06.999
key version, format version, sanitized
outcome, and request identifier.

20
00:01:06.999 --> 00:01:11.192
Avoid logging key bytes, plaintext data
keys, decrypted content, or

21
00:01:11.192 --> 00:01:14.897
full encrypted records and tags with
unnecessary sensitive context.

22
00:01:14.897 --> 00:01:17.432
Bound in-memory plaintext lifetime and
access.

23
00:01:17.432 --> 00:01:20.650
Treat decryption failure as a hard
boundary: never

24
00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:23.575
return provisional plaintext, continue a
protected action,

25
00:01:23.575 --> 00:01:26.890
or expose detailed cryptographic internals
to remote callers.

26
00:01:26.890 --> 00:01:30.205
Internal categories can support diagnosis
without disclosing secrets.

27
00:01:30.205 --> 00:01:33.520
Test tampered ciphertext, incorrect
associated data, wrong or

28
00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:37.421
unauthorized key versions, malformed
records, and unavailable key services.

29
00:01:37.421 --> 00:01:39.761
Confirm that authentication failure
returns no

30
00:01:39.761 --> 00:01:42.686
plaintext and causes no protected side
effect.

31
00:01:42.686 --> 00:01:45.416
Exercise old supported versions during
rotation and

32
00:01:45.416 --> 00:01:48.341
rejection of retired keys for new
encryption.

33
00:01:48.341 --> 00:01:52.632
Test nonce allocation across writers,
restarts, and relevant recovery paths.

34
00:01:52.632 --> 00:01:54.972
Check that logs and snapshots exclude

35
00:01:54.972 --> 00:01:57.897
secrets and that migrations preserve
authorized readability.

36
00:01:57.897 --> 00:02:01.017
These checks evaluate the wrapper and
lifecycle behavior

37
00:02:01.017 --> 00:02:04.625
around a vetted construction; they do not
establish the

38
00:02:04.625 --> 00:02:07.940
mathematical security of a home grown
cryptographic design.

